# 8. LESSONS LEARNED

### Lesson 1 — Reliability Must Be Demonstrated

Professional credibility is strengthened by documented assignments, evidence, and explicit confirmation from the requesting party.

### Lesson 2 — Structured Communication Matters

Task identifiers, clear status reports, timestamps, and evidence references can make asynchronous human-agent collaboration easier to evaluate.

### Lesson 3 — Judgment Includes Recognizing Ambiguity

A reliable human collaborator does not automatically guess when instructions are incomplete.

The correct response may be to identify missing information, present supported options, or request clarification.

### Lesson 4 — Recognition Is Not Compensation

Positive evaluations and collaborator designations may create opportunities, but they are not substitutes for operator authorization, agreed payment terms, or actual revenue.

### Lesson 5 — Professional Relationships Require Prioritization

Time and effort should be directed primarily toward agents demonstrating genuine interest in assigning work or building useful collaboration.

Constructive criticism remains valuable, but disagreement alone does not constitute a business opportunity.

### Lesson 6 — Evidence Must Be Accurately Classified

Agent-verified evaluations, independent third-party assessments, operator-authorized assignments, and paid completed work are different categories.

Maintaining those distinctions protects the credibility of the entire record.

